00:06:23,224 --> 00:06:28,191
I'm going to put a link to a paper called
resilient overlay networks that was from

80
00:06:28,191 --> 00:06:29,704
SOSP in 2001.

81
00:06:29,704 --> 00:06:34,966
And it's basically if you can put
an overlay on top of the Internet,

82
00:06:34,966 --> 00:06:37,181
let's see if this picture,

83
00:06:37,181 --> 00:06:42,651
let's say there's a network problem
somewhere right here, right?

84
00:06:42,651 --> 00:06:46,478
So in between this edge server and
this parent,

85
00:06:46,478 --> 00:06:50,394
the direct path in the Internet is broken.

86
00:06:50,394 --> 00:06:54,157
So what resilient overlay
networks does is, well,

87
00:06:54,157 --> 00:06:59,034
this edge server could have a path
to this parent server down here.

88
00:06:59,034 --> 00:07:04,417
This parent server could have a connection
to this parent, and now they've

89
00:07:04,417 --> 00:07:09,514
been able to bypass this network
issue that they have no control over.

90
00:07:09,514 --> 00:07:11,954
It's just the Internet.

91
00:07:11,954 --> 00:07:14,562
And so that's the concept of
resilient overlay networking.

92
00:07:14,562 --> 00:07:21,744
That's what they could take
advantage of with their network.

93
00:07:21,744 --> 00:07:26,672
They can also deal with packet
loss reduction or improve or

94
00:07:26,672 --> 00:07:32,520
reduce packet loss by using things
like forward error correction.

95
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:40,456
So putting extra information into
each packet which is not standard.

96
00:07:40,456 --> 00:07:44,992
The applications wouldn't understand it,
but if they could,

97
00:07:44,992 --> 00:07:50,368
as it's traversing the Internet,
put forward error correction onto it and

98
00:07:50,368 --> 00:07:55,324
then only locally strip that off,
that can improve the loss.

99
00:07:55,324 --> 00:07:59,525
They can optimize the transport
protocols like TCP,

100
00:07:59,525 --> 00:08:02,700
like pooling persistent connections,

101
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:07,340
doing retransmissions based
on network observations.

102
00:08:07,340 --> 00:08:09,764
So you can really optimize TCP.

103
00:08:09,764 --> 00:08:15,074
Actually that would be a pretty cool
